    summary: 'A hypervisor runs multiple guest operating systems on one machine, each with its own kernel, by using hardware virtualization extensions to run guest code directly on the CPU and trapping only privileged operations. On Linux, KVM is a kernel module that exposes `/dev/kvm`; a user-space VMM such as QEMU allocates guest memory, creates vCPU threads and emulates devices (disk, NIC, PCI bus) or hands real ones through. Nearly every cloud GPU instance is a VM of this kind.',
    purpose: 'Provides strong isolation (separate kernel, separate address spaces enforced by hardware) and lets one physical host be sold as several machines.',
    mechanics: 'Intel VT-x and AMD-V add a guest mode: the CPU executes guest instructions natively, and a VMCS/VMCB per vCPU records which events cause a VM exit into the hypervisor (privileged register writes, some MSRs, I/O port and MMIO accesses to emulated devices, external interrupts, EPT violations). KVM handles most exits inside the kernel (timers, interrupt injection, EPT faults); exits that need device emulation return to the VMM process via `ioctl(KVM_RUN)` returning to user space, where QEMU emulates the register access, at a cost of tens of microseconds. The type-1 vs type-2 distinction is largely historical: KVM is a module in a general-purpose kernel (type-2 in form) but runs guests with the same hardware assistance as ESXi or Xen (type-1); what matters for performance is which paths exit and how devices are provided. The guest kernel sees a virtual platform: emulated or paravirtualized (virtio) disk and NIC, a virtual APIC, a virtual PCI bus, and, with passthrough, real PCIe devices such as GPUs and NICs. virtio devices exchange data through shared-memory rings with the host (vhost in the kernel or vhost-user in user space), avoiding per-packet exits. Guest memory is host process memory (anonymous or hugetlbfs), mapped through nested page tables. Live migration, snapshots and overcommit of CPU and memory are hypervisor features; GPU passthrough disables most of them for that VM. The guest cannot see host-level metrics (other tenants, real NUMA layout unless the VMM exposes it), and its clock and scheduling are subject to the host.',
