    summary: 'A guest manages its own page tables mapping guest-virtual to guest-physical addresses; the hypervisor keeps a second table (Intel EPT, AMD NPT) mapping guest-physical to host-physical. The hardware walks both on a TLB miss, so the walk is two-dimensional and can touch up to 24 memory locations instead of 4. TLB hits are as fast as native; the penalty appears when the working set exceeds TLB reach, which is why large host and guest pages matter more inside a VM.',
    purpose: 'Lets the guest kernel manage memory as if it owned the hardware while the hypervisor enforces isolation and can relocate or overcommit guest memory.',
    mechanics: 'Each guest-virtual access first looks up the TLB, tagged by VPID/ASID so switches between guest and host do not flush it. On a miss the page walker reads the guest page table, but every guest-physical address it encounters (the table pointers themselves and the final frame) must itself be translated through EPT, so a 4-level guest walk with a 4-level EPT becomes a 2D walk of up to 4 × (4 + 1) + 4 = 24 references. Page-walk caches and the nested TLB absorb most of this, and measured overhead for TLB-friendly workloads is small; for memory-intensive workloads with random access over tens of GB, published measurements commonly show a few percent to low double-digit slowdown, workload dependent. The mitigation is to shrink both walks: back guest RAM with 2 MB or 1 GB host pages (hugetlbfs in QEMU, which also prevents the host from swapping it) so EPT entries are large, and use huge pages inside the guest (THP or hugetlbfs) so guest entries are large; with both, a TLB entry covers a large region and the walk shortens. EPT violations are exits raised when a guest-physical page has no host mapping yet; the first touch of guest memory faults through KVM, which allocates or maps the host page, so pre-allocating guest memory at boot avoids a fault storm when a model loads. The GPU has its own page tables for device memory and, for DMA into guest RAM, its accesses go through the IOMMU whose second-stage table mirrors EPT, so device DMA is not affected by the CPU-side 2D walk but does depend on the IOMMU mapping being large-page and pinned.',
