    summary: 'A container image carries CUDA libraries but cannot carry the kernel driver, and the user-mode driver (`libcuda.so`, `libnvidia-ml.so`, `libnvidia-ptxjitcompiler.so`) must match the kernel module exactly. The toolkit solves this at container start by bind-mounting the host\'s driver libraries, device nodes and utilities such as `nvidia-smi` into the container, selected by `NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` and `NVIDIA_DRIVER_CAPABILITIES`. It operates either as an OCI hook via the NVIDIA runtime or, in the current design, through CDI specifications that any CDI-aware runtime can apply.',
    purpose: 'Makes one image run on any host with a compatible driver, and gives per-container control over which GPUs are visible.',
    mechanics: 'With CDI, `nvidia-ctk cdi generate` inspects the host and writes a spec (`/etc/cdi/nvidia.yaml`) listing, per GPU and MIG device, the device nodes (`/dev/nvidia0`, `/dev/nvidiactl`, `/dev/nvidia-uvm`, `/dev/nvidia-modeset`), the driver library files to mount, and hooks (for example updating the ld cache); a pod or `docker run --device nvidia.com/gpu=0` references those names and containerd or the CRI edits the OCI spec accordingly. In the legacy path, the `nvidia-container-runtime` wraps runc and adds a `prestart` hook that calls `nvidia-container-cli` to do the same mounts based on `NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES` (`all`, indices, UUIDs, or MIG ids like `MIG-GPU-.../1/0`) and `NVIDIA_DRIVER_CAPABILITIES` (`compute`, `utility`, `video`, `graphics`, `display`). The mounted `libcuda.so` is the host driver\'s, so the container\'s CUDA runtime (cudart, from the image) must be supported by that driver: each driver version reports a maximum CUDA version, and an image built with a newer toolkit fails at initialization with a driver-too-old error. NVIDIA\'s forward-compatibility packages (`cuda-compat`) let some newer CUDA runtimes run on older data-center driver branches by shipping a newer user-mode driver in the container, subject to hardware and branch restrictions that are documented per release and should be treated as an exception rather than the default. Minor-version compatibility means images within the same CUDA major generally work on any driver that supports that major. The toolkit does not virtualize anything: device nodes are host device nodes, and the process talks to `nvidia.ko` through ioctls like any host process; isolation between containers on one GPU is whatever MIG, MPS or compute mode provides. `nvidia-smi` inside the container shows only the selected devices.',
