    subsystem: 'virt',
    modes: ['system', 'isolation', 'scheduling'],
    aliases: ['NVIDIA device plugin', 'k8s-device-plugin', 'GPU operator', 'extended resource', 'DRA', 'dynamic resource allocation'],
    keywords: ['nvidia.com/gpu', 'nvidia.com/mig-1g.10gb', 'kubelet', 'ListAndWatch', 'Allocate', 'node feature discovery', 'GPU feature discovery', 'topology manager', 'DCGM exporter', 'time-slicing config'],
    summary: 'Kubernetes does not know what a GPU is; the device plugin API lets a vendor daemon on each node report countable devices under a resource name such as `nvidia.com/gpu`, and the kubelet hands specific device ids to a container at start. The NVIDIA plugin reports one unit per GPU by default, or one per MIG instance, or N replicas per GPU for time-slicing, and at allocation time sets the environment or CDI edits that make those devices visible. Dynamic Resource Allocation (DRA) is the newer, more expressive API (structured parameters, claims) and is generation dependent in Kubernetes versions.',
    purpose: 'Lets the scheduler place pods on nodes with free GPUs and binds specific devices to containers without the pod knowing node details.',
    mechanics: 'The plugin runs as a DaemonSet, registers with the kubelet over a Unix socket, and streams `ListAndWatch` responses listing device ids and health; the kubelet exposes the count in node capacity (`nvidia.com/gpu: 8`). A pod requests `resources.limits: nvidia.com/gpu: 2` (requests must equal limits for extended resources; fractional values are not allowed), the scheduler filters nodes by free count, and on start the kubelet calls `Allocate` with chosen ids; the plugin returns environment (`NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=GPU-uuid,...`) or CDI device names that the runtime and toolkit turn into mounts. Whole-GPU allocation means one pod holds the device exclusively, even if it uses a fraction. For fractional use the plugin supports two configurations: MIG strategies (`single` or `mixed`) that expose each MIG instance as a device, either as `nvidia.com/gpu` uniformly or as `nvidia.com/mig-1g.10gb`-style names; and time-slicing, where a config declares `replicas: N` so one GPU appears as N schedulable units that share it without memory or compute isolation (the plugin can also enable MPS for such replicas). GPU Feature Discovery and Node Feature Discovery label nodes with product, driver, CUDA and MIG capability so pods can use node selectors. The kubelet topology manager, with `single-numa-node` policy, aligns GPU, CPU and NIC allocations to one NUMA node when the plugin reports topology hints. The GPU Operator installs the driver, toolkit, plugin, DCGM exporter and MIG manager as a bundle. Health checks mark a device unhealthy on Xid events so the scheduler stops placing pods on it. Device plugin allocation is static for the pod\'s lifetime; there is no migration or preemption of GPU work.',
